course: dictionary
lesson: 7686
title: One word, a long history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a story. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «Today Catholics and Protestants both admit faults from that time.» Catholics and Protestants are two groups, and they are talking about a time when they disagreed, so Protestant names a group of Christians.
explain: Look for the capital letter too. A capital P shows a name for a group of people or churches, not a word for someone who argues. «The Reformation split western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches.» Here the sentence tells you the Protestant churches came from a split, so they are a separate group.
explain: Some words change their job in a sentence. Protestant can be a noun, as in «A Protestant is a Christian who...», or an adjective, as in «Many Protestant and Anglican churches have women ministers.» Here Protestant describes the churches. Ask yourself: is it naming a person, or describing a thing?
explain: Be careful and respectful with words about faith. In history lessons you may read that Martin Luther «protested against some practices of the Church in his time.» Protest here means speaking out against something you think is wrong. When context is not enough, a dictionary or your teacher can help.
fact: A Protestant is a Christian who belongs to a church that broke away from the Roman Catholic Church at the Reformation, or grew from those churches.
fact: As an adjective, Protestant describes those churches and their beliefs, as in «Protestant church».
fact: Protestant churches include Lutheran, Anglican, Presbyterian, Methodist, Baptist and Pentecostal churches.
fact: Protestants usually stress the Bible, faith and prayer in your own language, and most do not have a pope.
fact: In Northern Ireland, Protestant and Catholic are linked to old conflicts, so they are said carefully and respectfully.
exercise: «The Reformation split western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches.» What is a Protestant? | A person who always says no to everything | *A Christian who belongs to a church that broke away from the Roman Catholic Church, or grew from those churches | A member of a palace guard | A church bell ringer
exercise: «Many Protestant and Anglican churches have women ministers and bishops.» What part of speech is Protestant in this sentence? | Noun, because it names a person | *Adjective, because it describes the churches | Adverb, because it tells how something happens | Verb, because it shows an action
exercise: «Today Catholics and Protestants both admit faults from that time.» Find and copy one word that names a group of Christians. | *Catholics | time | faults | admit
test: In history, Martin Luther «protested against some practices of the Church in his time.» What does protested mean here? | *Spoke out against something he thought was wrong | Cheered loudly for the Church | Ate a large meal | Sang a song in the street
test: Why are the words Protestant and Catholic said carefully in Northern Ireland? | They are rude words that nobody should say | They are only used for names of cakes | They mean the same as a pope | *They are linked to old conflicts, so people use them respectfully
exam: Which is true about most Protestants? | *They stress the Bible, faith and prayer in your own language | They never pray | They all have a pope who lives in Rome | They believe every church must be the same as the Catholic Church
